  "analysis": "I've read the statute file. It contains tax brackets for different filing statuses, personal exemptions (§151), standard deduction (§163), and dependency rules (§152). I need to calculate Alice and Bob's 2017 tax based on these rules.",
  "plan": "Let me analyze the facts:\n1. Total income: Alice ($133,200) + Bob ($44,311) = $177,511\n2. Standard deduction for married filing jointly: $12,700 (from §163(b)(2)(A))\n3. Additional standard deduction for blind taxpayer: $600 (from §163(f)(2)(A)) - Alice has been blind since 2014\n4. Total standard deduction: $13,300\n5. Personal exemptions: $2,000 each (§151(d)(1))\n   - Alice and Bob qualify: $4,000\n   - Charlie: Need to check if dependent. The gross income test requires gross income under $4,050 (from §152(c)(1)(B)). Charlie's income from work is $4,525, which exceeds $4,050, so Charlie is NOT a dependent.\n6. Total exemptions: $4,000\n7. Taxable income: $177,511 - $13,300 - $4,000 = $160,211\n8. Tax calculation for married filing jointly (from §1(a)): $35,928.50 + 36% of excess over $140,000\n   - Excess: $160,211 - $140,000 = $20,211\n   - Tax: $35,928.50 + (0.36 × $20,211) = $35,928.50 + $7,275.96 = $43,204.46\n9. Rounded to whole number: $43,204",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mkdir -p /app/output\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo 'Answer: 43204' > /app/output/answer.txt\n",
